 be difficult for these  family members to sit through although  they have wanted to see some sort of  justice for a long time almost six years  it'll be more than six years by the time  george wagner iv  goes to trial i know that during the or  after the plea hearing for jake wagner  uh the family members seem very happy  he's going to prison for the rest of his  life they didn't seem quite as happy  after angela wagner pleaded guilty  because she's eligible for release after  serving 30 years all right we'll see how  these pike county massacre cases  continue as adam said it's months and  months out maybe there's a plea maybe  it's a trout we'll see how that ends up  thank you all coming up on law and crime  daily day four of the waffle house  shooting trial hear first hand letters  the defendant wrote to pop icon taylor  swift plus a shocking verdict is reached  in the andre cole murder trial out of  ohio all that and more still ahead on  law and crime daily  [Music]  welcome back an ohio veteran on trial  for murder is now a free woman after a  not guilty verdict from the jury audrey  cole was charged with murder and  felonious assault in the shooting death  of her boyfriend matthew mott last year  the two were both military veterans and  had domestic issues in the past in may  2021 the couple allegedly got into an  argument over their three-year-old  daughter taking medication cole said  mott beat her and threw her head into a  wall before she shot and killed him with  an ar-15 she kept under her bed cole  said she shot him in the hallway of  their home in self-defense the jury must  have believed her attorneys as they  found her not guilty of both charges  after just four hours of deliberating  once the jury was done deciding  wednesday afternoon judge rappella read  the verdict  at this time the jury has reached  unanimous verdicts on both counts  i will read them individually  as for count one that is the charge of  murder  the jury has found the defendant not  guilty  they have made the finding that the  state of ohio did not prove beyond a  reasonable doubt all of the elements of  murder as charged in count one of the  indictment as for count two felonious  assault  the jury found the defendant not guilty  therefore it is a not guilty finding on  both counts  criminal defense attorney adam conte and  co-host terry austin are back to discuss  the self-defense acquittal of audrey  cole terry if you blinked you missed  this trial it was all done in what like  two and a half days why do you think the  jury found her not guilty so quickly  you know we don't get many not guilty  verdicts but i think the defense was  able to show that cole actually did fear  for her life she had had serious bodily  injury before he previously beat her up  while she was pregnant and so i think  that fact worked in her favor and the  husband was twice her size the defense  was  pointing out during its summation that  ohio has um stand your ground law and  you don't have to retreat and i think  that too was another reason that they  came to the conclusion so quickly adam  that's the conclusion let's talk about  how we got there are you surprised  because i know some people are  that the defense won a self-defense case  without putting on their client or a  single witness  in a vacuum yes i would be surprised the  facts of this case though i think they  they nailed it i mean the prosecution  had very little evidence the fact that  from jury selection to verdict is under  three days is unheard of for a  misdemeanor shoplifting case let alone a  murder case i mean there was an evidence  there and the evidence that was there  was really sort of in her favor so  although normally i would say yeah  self-defense you got to get up there you  got to really explain it you got to show  the jury why you were in fear  this just wasn't that case and and and  thankfully the jury got it right yeah i  think to the defense's credit uh while  she didn't take the stand as we just saw  there a moment ago her interrogation  video and her 9-1-1 call kind of put it  all out there so maybe she didn't need  to take the stand her story was already  there uh but of course it could also  just be the jury thought you shouldn't  be able to beat up your girlfriend like  that and just acquitted her of that but  we'll never know we'll see how it plays  out thank you both when we come back  inside the man of the accused waffle  house shooter written letters from the  travis reign king are read in court as  defense attorneys say he grappled with  delusion what famous celebrities he  called on for help  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily on  day four of the waffle house shooting  trial celebrity taylor swift takes  center stage as the defense alleges  suspect travis ranking had a delusional  relationship with a pop star  prosecutors say ranking walked into a  waffle house in nashville tennessee and  opened fire in 2018 four people were  killed and others were injured after a  massive 34-hour manhunt involving almost  every three-letter agency in the country  ranking was taken into custody the trial  was put on hold for months as ranking  was treated for schizophrenia a judge  later deemed him fit to stand trial  defense attorneys are arguing insanity  saying ranking had severe mental health  issues at the time of the shooting on  wednesday travis rankings or  hand-written letters were read on the  stand a detective told jurors he  addressed the letters to taylor swift  and oprah winfrey claiming what ranking  called a cruel relationship was swiff  taylor i'm really confused right now i  don't know if it was you in the pictures  on instagram or someone who looks like  you do you really have a twin sister  which one am i in love with then  i want the tailor that wrote those  beautiful songs and sang to me at her  concert  maybe taylor 1 wrote half and taylor 2  wrote half  oprah i'm writing you because i'm in  love with taylor swift and i don't know  if i'm delusional or not i started  writing taylor june 2nd 2015.  the letters were about dreams that i was  having i saw taylor for the first time  september 29th 2015.  it was at a concert and she mouthed the  words hello to me while she was singing  a song  after that i started to  after that i started to notice that what  i was saying to her was getting repeated  back to me on the internet  also things that i was doing on my phone  and computer  i wrote her and gave her permission to  do those things because she was being  nice to me and i didn't want her to feel  guilty  later i took back permission for her to  do that because she started being  cruel and lying about everything  she catfished me on the internet made me  fall in love with her and then started  being  nasty to  me adam starr witnesses and medical  examiners have testified how would you  rather measure the prosecution's case in  saying the insanity of defense doesn't  apply here  well it's tricky you know i was watching  the testimony earlier of this and of  their expert witness  and  i i think i think the prosecution has  done a good job in trying to poke holes  in it by showing uh areas where he was  really competent like him packing his  backpack with silver bars and it was  really sort of a plan sort of it seemed  premeditated and planned by a cognizant  mind uh but i mean listen you listen to  those letters and those letters are  obviously incredibly helpful for the  defense so it's just it's such a back  and forth right now it's so hard to put  on an insanity defense i think the  prosecution's going to get the  conviction but it's been a very  interesting trial  interesting to say the least terry let's  look at it from the other side is there  a chance for the defense to win an  insanity defense argument and what do  you think they need to prove to the jury  in order to get there  well under tennessee law what they'd  have to do is show that at the time of  the offense  he did not know what he was doing he had  a severe mental illness and it caused  him to not understand the consequences  of his actions honestly i do agree with  adam you know and they have to show that  through clear and convincing evidence i  don't think they're going to be able to  show this because there's so much  evidence that he did understand what was  going on he knew the difference between  right and wrong he tried to hide after  the fact he ran from the scene he took  his clothes off he had a change of  clothes so i think that at the end of  the day the prosecution is going to be  able to show that while he might have a  mental illness or defect he knew what he  was doing and he knew the consequences  of his actions so he would be found  guilty all right we'll see how that  plays out in closing arguments as they  tie it all together thank you both for  joining us always a pleasure and thank  you for joining us here on law and crime  daily we'll see you next time as we  discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

